2026-05-12 20:34:06,366 - INFO - thoughts: ['Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering for the given dataset to help improve the downstream model performance in predicting forest cover type:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: **Create a terrain ruggedness index**\nThe "terrain ruggedness index" captures the variability in elevation and slope in the local area. This could help the model associate steep and rugged terrains with specific forest types.\n\n```python\n# Terrain_Ruggedness\n# Feature description: Combines slope and elevation to proxy terrain ruggedness. Calculated as √(Slope² + Elevation²).\n# Usefulness: Ruggedness typically impacts accessibility, vegetation, and soil conditions, which are related to forest cover type.\ndf[\'Terrain_Ruggedness\'] = (df[\'Slope\']**2 + df[\'Elevation\']**2)**0.5\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: **Calculate distance to water slope (hydrology slope effect)**\n\nThe difference in elevation divided by the horizontal distance to hydrology gives an effective slope leading to water features. This may be important for distinguishing vegetation types close to such features.\n\n```python\n# Hydrology_Slope_Effect\n# Feature description: Ratio of Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology to Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: Helps contextualize gradients and flow paths near water, which could indicate unique growth environments for specific forest types.\ndf[\'Hydrology_Slope_Effect\'] = df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] / (df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: **Create a cosine transformation of Aspect**\n\nAspect (azimuth) often influences sunlight exposure, which affects vegetation growth. A cosine transformation can better reflect north-south slopes and the degree of exposure.\n\n```python\n# Aspect_Cosine\n# Feature description: Cosine of the Aspect feature to emphasize directional exposure relative to sunlight.\n# Usefulness: Reflects the influence of sun positioning (e.g., north-facing vs. south-facing slopes) on vegetation and water retention patterns.\ndf[\'Aspect_Cosine\'] = np.cos(np.radians(df[\'Aspect\']))\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Additional Note: Dropping columns (if necessary)\nIf certain columns are irrelevant due to lack of variability, constant values, or are redundant for the model, they can be dropped to avoid adding noise.\n\n```python\n# Explanation: Drop constant-valued soil types as they contribute no variability to the model.\nconstant_soil_cols = [\'Soil_Type1\', \'Soil_Type5\', \'Soil_Type14\', \'Soil_Type15\', \'Soil_Type37\']\ndf.drop(columns=constant_soil_cols, inplace=True)\n```end\n\n', "```python\n# Feature name: Elevation_bin\n# Feature description: Binned elevation values into categories based on terrain features (low, medium, high elevation).\n# Usefulness: Binning elevation helps capture non-linear relationships that may exist in forest cover types. For example, certain tree species may prefer lower versus higher altitudes, and this transformation introduces categorical information important for the model.\n\nimport pandas as pd\ndf['Elevation_bin'] = pd.cut(df['Elevation'], bins=[2000, 2900, 3100, 3700], labels=['Low', 'Medium', 'High'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: Hydrology_Distance_Ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology to Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: This ratio gives a better indication of proximity and relative steepness between a cell and its nearest hydrological feature. For example, areas with steep slopes close to water bodies might have distinct vegetation that thrives in such conditions.\n\ndf['Hydrology_Distance_Ratio'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] / (df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: Combined_Hillshade_Avg\n# Feature description: The average of the three hillshade values (Hillshade_9am, Hillshade_Noon, Hillshade_3pm).\n# Usefulness: Combining hillshade values into an average captures a single measure of sunlight exposure throughout the day. Areas receiving higher or lower cumulative sunlight can influence the type of vegetation present.\n\ndf['Combined_Hillshade_Avg'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] + df['Hillshade_Noon'] + df['Hillshade_3pm']) / 3\n```end\n\nDropping irrelevant or redundant columns based on their utility:\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Soil_Type1 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type1'], inplace=True)\n# Soil_Type1 has no variance (all values are 0), providing no additional information for the model.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Wilderness_Area4 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\n# Wilderness_Area4 has an extremely low mean value (0.0054) and very low variance, indicating it has minimal influence on the model's prediction since almost all entries are 0.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Aspect is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect'], inplace=True)\n# Aspect (degree azimuth) is less informative in its raw form due to its cyclical nature. Transformations such as sine/cosine of the angle are better suited to capture its influence on terrain and vegetation.\n```end"]
