ng the given operators, with reasoning for how they will improve the model's ability to predict forest cover type.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1: Add `Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology`\n```python\n# Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Difference between Elevation and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology, representing how high above the hydrology the cell is.\n# Usefulness: Provides environmental context to determine forest cover; areas closer or above water sources are more likely to exhibit specific vegetation. This informs hydrology-driven vegetation trends.\n\ndf['Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology'] = df['Elevation'] - df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']\n```end\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Add `Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features`\n```python\n# Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features\n# Feature description: Ratio between Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points to capture relative proximity to human development versus wildfire risks.\n# Usefulness: Balances the influence of human intervention (roads) against natural disaster risk (fire points). Dense forests closer to fire points may present a unique pattern compared to those near roads.\n\ndf['Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)\n```end\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Add `Slope_Effect_Hydrology`\n```python\n# Slope_Effect_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Product of slope and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology, representing how steeply the cell is oriented toward or away from hydrology.\n# Usefulness: Steep slopes and proximity to water often dictate soil erosion, vegetation density, and forest distribution. Adds geomorphological context useful in forest type prediction.\n\ndf['Slope_Effect_Hydrology'] = df['Slope'] * abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Feature Dropping: Remove Redundant or Low-Impact Columns\n\n#### Drop `Soil_Type5`, `Soil_Type14`, and `Soi