### Step 1: Aspect Transformation
python
# Feature name: Aspect_Sine
# Feature description: Sine of the Aspect angle to capture directional influence. Aspect values correspond to azimuth directions, and the transformed value will allow better modeling of north-facing vs. south-facing slopes' effects on vegetation and forest cover type.
# Usefulness: South-facing slopes (higher sine values) are typically warmer and can influence soil moisture or vegetation, while north-facing slopes (lower values) may be cooler and shaded. By taking the sine of the Aspect, we can better reflect these terrain characteristics in the model.

df['Aspect_Sine'] = np.sin(np.radians(df['Aspect']))
end

---

### Step 2: Combine Elevation and Hydrology Interaction
python
# Feature name: Hydrology_Elevation_Ratio
# Feature description: Ratio of Elevation to Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology. This captures the relative steepness of terrain in relation to hydrology features. Areas higher above water sources may have different soil moisture and vegetation characteristics compared to areas closer to hydrology features.
# Usefulness: Elevation and proximity to water sources have significant influences on soil moisture and tree types. Modeling this ratio helps capture hydrological-persistent patterns related to forest cover.

df['Hydrology_Elevation_Ratio'] = df['Elevation'] / (df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small constant to avoid division by zero
end

---

### Step 3: Capture Local Brightness Contrast
python
# Feature name: Hillshade_Difference_Normalized
# Feature description: Hillshade difference between 9am and 3pm, normalized by the Noon hillshade. This measures the terrain's light exposure dynamics and surface shadow contrast throughout the day.
# Usefulness: Normalized difference helps to model variations in terrain brightness over time. Forest cover type may prefer flatter terrains with high light exposure or favor shade-adapted areas in steep shadowed gradients.

df['Hillshade_Difference_Normalized'] = (df['Hillshade_9am'] - df['Hillshade_3pm']) / (df['Hillshade_Noon'] + 1e-6)  # Avoid division by zero
end

---

### Optional Column Drops:
While they aren't strictly necessary, dropping redundant or highly sparse columns may improve model runtime or reduce overfitting.

python
# Explanation why the column Soil_Type36 is dropped
# Soil_Type36 has an extremely sparse presence (Mean = 0.0001), indicating that it contributes minimal variation to forest cover predictions. Dropping it can reduce noise without sacrificing meaningful information.

df.drop(columns=['Soil_Type36'], inplace=True)
end 

python
# Explanation why the column Roadway_Firepoint_Proximity is dropped
# The feature exhibits extreme variability, including excessively high numeric outliers that may overwhelm model learning. Its impact appears less relevant given other distance-based variables like Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.

df.drop(columns=['Roadway_Firepoint_Proximity'], inplace=True)
end
