4 is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Wilderness_Area4'], inplace=True)\n# Wilderness_Area4 has an extremely low mean value (0.0054) and very low variance, indicating it has minimal influence on the model's prediction since almost all entries are 0.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Aspect is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Aspect'], inplace=True)\n# Aspect (degree azimuth) is less informative in its raw form due to its cyclical nature. Transformations such as sine/cosine of the angle are better suited to capture its influence on terrain and vegetation.\nend", "### Feature Engineering: Adding Useful Columns  \nBelow are three possible next steps leveraging the given operators, with reasoning for how they will improve the model's ability to predict forest cover type.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1: Add Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology\npython\n# Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Difference between Elevation and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology, representing how high above the hydrology the cell is.\n# Usefulness: Provides environmental context to determine forest cover; areas closer or above water sources are more likely to exhibit specific vegetation. This informs hydrology-driven vegetation trends.\n\ndf['Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology'] = df['Elevation'] - df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology']\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Add Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features\npython\n# Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features\n# Feature description: Ratio between Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points to capture relative proximity to human development versus wildfire risks.\n# Usefulness: Balances the influence of human intervention (roads) against natural disaster risk (fire points). Dense forests closer to fire points may present a unique pattern compared to those near roads.\n\ndf['Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features'] = df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] / (df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] + 1e-6)\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Add Slope_Effect_Hydrology\npython\n# Slope_Effect_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Product of slope and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology, representing how steeply the cell is oriented toward or away from hydrology.\n# Usefulness: Steep slopes and proximity to water often dictate soil erosion, vegetation density, and forest distribution. Adds geomorphological context useful in forest type prediction.\n\ndf['Slope_Effect_Hydrology'] = df['Slope'] * abs(df['Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Feature Dropping: Remove Redundant or Low-Impact Columns\n\n#### Drop Soil_Type5, Soil_Type14, and Soil_Type15\npython\n# Explanation why the columns are dropped\n# Soil_Type5, Soil_Type14, and Soil_Type15 are dropped as they are entirely zero across all samples (no variance). These columns do not contribute any information for distinguishing patterns in forest cover.\ndf.drop(columns=['Soil_Type5', 'Soil_Type14', 'Soil_Type15'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Feature engineering to improve predictions:\n\n1. Combining Soil Type Indicators into a Single Categorical Feature:\npython\n# Combined_Soil_Type\n# Feature description: Combines all Soil_Type indicators into a single categorical feature representing unique soil types.\n# Usefulness: Consolidates 40 binary features into a single categorical feature, potentially helping the model focus on patterns across soil types. Reduces dimensionality and makes soil types easier to interpret downstream.\ndf['Combined_Soil_Type'] = df.loc[:, [f'Soil_Type{i}' for i in range(1, 41)]].idxmax(axis=1)\ndf['Combined_Soil_Type'] = df['Combined_Soil_Type'].str.replace('Soil_Type', '', regex=False)\ndf['Combined_Soil_Type'] = df['Combined_Soil_Type'].astype(int)\nend\n\n2. Interaction Between Slope and Hillshade Variables:\npython\n# Slope_Hillshade_Interaction\n# Feature description: Product of Slope and Combined_Hillshade_Avg to capture the combined effect of terrain steepness and sunlight exposure.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines the terrain characteristics (Slope) and illumination from sunlight (Hillshade), which might correlate with vegetation type and hence forest cover type.\ndf['Slope_Hillshade_Interaction'] = df['Slope'] * df['Combined_Hillshade_Avg']\nend\n\n3. Log Transformation of Horizontal Distances to Reduce Skewness:\npython\n# Log_Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, Log_Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points, Log_Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\n# Feature description: Log transformation of Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology.\n# Usefulness: Horizontal distance features exhibit large ranges and potential skewness. Log transformations reduce skewness, making the data easier for the model to learn.\ndf['Log_Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'] = np.log1p(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways'])\ndf['Log_Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'] = np.log1p(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points'])\ndf['Log_Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'] = np.log1p(df['Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Columns to drop (if necessary):\npython\n# Explanation why the Soil_Type indicators are dropped\n# The individual binary Soil_Type indicators are redundant after creating the Combined_Soil_Type feature.\ndf.drop(columns=[f'Soil_Type{i}' for i in range(1, 41)], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features is dropped\n# This column has extreme values (Mean = 134683.0145, Std = 18969391.5661) and outliers, making it less interpretable and potentially noisy for the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Reasons for Feature Selection:\n- Combining soil types reduces dimensionality and groups soil types logically.\n- Creating interaction terms captures nonlinear relationships between terrain and sunlight, which influence vegetation.\n- Applying log transformations ensures model robustness by reducing feature skewness. These alterations improve interpretability while aligning the dataset with the real-world context of forest cover prediction."), with improvements -
