red\n# Feature description: Square the Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology feature to amplify its effect for instances with significant differences in elevation relative to hydrological features.\n# Usefulness: The squared term increases emphasis on extreme elevation differences, which could correlate strongly with vegetation growth patterns, hydrological flow, and ecological differences within the forest.\n\ndf['Relative_Elevation_Squared'] = df['Relative_Elevation_to_Hydrology'] ** 2\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: Normalized_Terrain_Ruggedness\n# Feature description: Apply min-max scaling to Terrain_Ruggedness_Index to normalize its range and ensure consistent interaction with other scaled features in the model.\n# Usefulness: Terrain ruggedness is an important ecological factor. Normalizing it ensures that it contributes evenly to downstream predictions alongside other normalized features like slope or hillshade values. This helps prevent disproportionate influence by features with differing ranges.\n\ndf['Normalized_Terrain_Ruggedness'] = (df['Terrain_Ruggedness_Index'] - df['Terrain_Ruggedness_Index'].min()) / (df['Terrain_Ruggedness_Index'].max() - df['Terrain_Ruggedness_Index'].min())\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features is dropped\n# This column has an extreme range ([0.0, 4609000000.0]) with an enormous standard deviation (18969391.5661). \n# It indicates potential outliers or disproportionate scaling that could negatively influence the model. Furthermore, \n# the normalized distance features are better captured via other interactions or transformations, rendering this column redundant.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['Normalized_Horizontal_Distance_To_Features'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Hydrology_Distance_Ratio is dropped\n# This column exhibits peculiar values, with a vast range from negative extreme (-918.000918000918) to an extreme positive (947000000.0). \n# The high standard deviation suggests it may introduce noise or distort model behavior due to inaccuracies or inconsistencies \n# in the way ratios are calculated from hydrology distances compared to vertical distances.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['Hydrology_Distance_Ratio'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why redundant soil-type columns are dropped (e.g., Soil_Type36, Soil_Type8)\n# Any soil type column with a mean near 0 and negligible variance (e.g., Soil_Type36 mean=0.0001, std=0.0095) adds little information \n# to the model due to its near-absence in the dataset. These columns dilute the model performance without meaningful contribution.\n\nsoil_types_to_drop = ['Soil_Type36', 'Soil_Type8', 'Soil_Type7', 'Soil_Type6', 'Soil_Type3']\ndf.drop(columns=soil_types_to_drop, inplace=True)\n```end"]
